  "snp3.6:23.1": "",
  "snp3.6:24.1": "I think Norman’s reconstruction of this to root <i lang='pi' translate='no'>ñā</i> (followed by Bodhi) is too speculative. The word makes fine sense as is, and is more metaphorically connected with the sense of <i lang='pi' translate='no'>khetta</i>. ",
  "snp3.6:24.2": "",
  "snp3.6:24.3": "",
  "snp3.6:24.4": "",
  "snp3.6:24.5": "",
  "snp3.6:25.1": "Reading <i lang='pi' translate='no'>vijeyya</i>; both <i lang='pi' translate='no'>viceyya</i> and <i lang='pi' translate='no'>vijeyya</i> are accepted in the commentary. The “fields” are the “fields of deeds” (<a href='https://suttacentral.net/an3.77/en/sujato#2.3'>AN 3.77:2.3</a>). ",
  "snp3.6:25.2": "",
  "snp3.6:25.3": "",
  "snp3.6:25.4": "",
  "snp3.6:25.5": "",
  "snp3.6:26.1": "",
  "snp3.6:26.2": "",
  "snp3.6:26.3": "",
  "snp3.6:26.4": "",
  "snp3.6:27.1": "<i lang='pi' translate='no'>Paṇḍarāni</i> means not “senses” per commentary, but is a synonym for <i lang='pi' translate='no'>suddhi</i> in the next line. A true <i lang='pi' translate='no'>paṇḍita</i> is pure both inside and out. ",
  "snp3.6:27.2": "",
  "snp3.6:27.3": "",
  "snp3.6:27.4": "",
